WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.580
Sometimes the act of pushing other
distractions away can help us to focus.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.370
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.880
Today, I want to talk about focus.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.340
How do we stay focused
in language learning?

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:19.040
Uh, remember, if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, uh, click on the bell.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.990
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.280
If you follow me on Spotify or
Apple Podcasts or elsewhere,

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.090
please leave a review.

00:00:27.479 --> 00:00:29.040
Um, focus.

00:00:29.549 --> 00:00:31.020
I get this question all the time.

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.000
People say they lose focus.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.730
So how do we, how do we get focused?

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.660
How do we stay focused?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.105
Um, it just turns out that in my
Persian learning, Uh, we were able to

00:00:42.105 --> 00:00:48.825
get permission to use some podcasts by
a fellow called B Plus who, uh, does

00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:55.215
sort of Farsi, Persian language, uh,
summaries of very popular self-help books.

00:00:55.545 --> 00:00:59.785
So he did one on, uh, deep
learning uh deep work...

00:01:01.135 --> 00:01:03.535
deep work, and one on focus...

00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:06.705
Uh, and so I went back
to have a look at that.

00:01:07.515 --> 00:01:10.304
I thought I wanted to see if there
was something there and I had gone

00:01:10.304 --> 00:01:14.054
through them in Persian and didn't
find them that interesting actually.

00:01:14.085 --> 00:01:17.265
And so, but then I went back and looked
at them again to look at them again, to

00:01:17.265 --> 00:01:18.914
see if I could learn anything relevant.

00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:22.995
Uh, right away I was more focused
cause I was interested in the

00:01:22.995 --> 00:01:26.175
subject to see if there was anything
there that would be helpful in

00:01:26.355 --> 00:01:27.855
addressing this whole issue of focus.

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:32.235
There wasn't because like so many of
these self-help books, it's just a lot

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:38.265
of common sense stuff packaged together
in a new format, you know: we need

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:43.275
to concentrate, we need to focus, uh,
and you know, taking some examples of

00:01:43.275 --> 00:01:48.090
people in history who would isolate
themselves for two weeks at a time.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:53.110
Bill Gates would go off to
his cottage or Jung the famous

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.750
psychologist or psychiatrist had a
chalet in Switzerland or something.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:04.440
And, and certainly I remember, um, you
know, there are people in history, uh,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.390
who was it, who had his tower that he...

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.850
Montaigne  who wrote essays in French.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.000
He had his tower there somewhere down
in the Southwest of France where he

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.440
would go and he would be isolated.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.370
And obviously if we can isolate
ourselves, that helps us concentrate.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.549
But most people who are learning
languages, they can't take two

00:02:20.549 --> 00:02:22.440
weeks off to go off somewhere.

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:25.410
If they have two weeks, they're
probably better off to go to the

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.000
country where the language is spoken.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:29.299
So how do we focus in
on language learning?

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:35.540
Um, some so, and, and also, and this
comes up in both of these books and

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.995
it's something we're well aware of, the
modern sort of internet information age,

00:02:41.025 --> 00:02:46.245
where we have access to information, we
have access to so much language content.

00:02:46.245 --> 00:02:50.114
For example, we have access to,
you know, grammatically grammatical

00:02:50.114 --> 00:02:55.635
explanations at the click of a
Google, uh, you know, uh, keystroke.

00:02:56.144 --> 00:03:01.394
Um, and we have also access to, to an
audience for anything we want to do.

00:03:01.394 --> 00:03:03.435
Like right now I'm talking
to people I don't know.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.280
So this is a new world and
there's good and there's bad.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.650
And the good is, yeah, we can access
so many things and we can get our

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.830
messages, so many people, and we
can learn so many things quickly.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.760
If I, if I'm doing something in
Persian and they refer to a certain

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.080
dynasty and the history of Persia,
then I can go and click Wikipedia

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.260
and, and read it in English, which
is easier for me to understand.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:27.980
Then I go back.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.380
And so there are all these distractions.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.040
So what do we do?

00:03:32.565 --> 00:03:38.385
First of all, one very surprising thing is
I find that one of the best places for me

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:43.935
to focus is in an airplane where there's
noise there's people, but I'm stuck there.

00:03:44.565 --> 00:03:49.515
So in a way, all of the noise
and the possible distractions

00:03:49.545 --> 00:03:50.565
are not distractions.

00:03:50.565 --> 00:03:54.225
I find that I'm able to do a lot of
reading and a lot of study on an airplane.

00:03:55.185 --> 00:04:00.090
Uh, we had a programmer at
LingQ who did his best work...

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.989
he, we have a sort of what we call the
sky train, which is our public transit

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:06.359
system, which travels around above ground.

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:07.500
It's not underground.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.130
And he would go on the sky train with
all of the distractions that were

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.910
there and he found himself better
able to concentrate and come up with

00:04:15.089 --> 00:04:17.769
solutions in terms of programming.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.390
So it's not obvious.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.500
And I have said before that,
if I sit down, I want to listen

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.010
to something just to listen to
something I easily lose my focus.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.700
Whereas if I'm doing the dishes
or in the car, I have some other

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.805
activity, uh, I'm better able to focus.

00:04:32.895 --> 00:04:37.515
I know there are people who can
focus better in a, in a cafe with

00:04:37.515 --> 00:04:40.725
all the distractions and the other
people than they can, kind of, at

00:04:40.725 --> 00:04:42.165
home where they're on their own.

00:04:42.495 --> 00:04:49.005
So sometimes the act of pushing other
distractions away can help us to focus.

00:04:49.335 --> 00:04:52.605
I think everyone has to find
their own, you know, most,

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:56.415
you know, which environment is
more, most conducive to focus.

00:04:57.285 --> 00:05:02.670
Uh, I think having a time
of the day, uh, is good.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.730
So my listening is not
so much a focus activity.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.580
I listen, I sometimes focus
sometimes don't focus.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:09.960
I don't think it's a problem.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.210
It's, it's free learning.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.360
Cause it's a learning that happens
while I'm doing something else.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.410
So I'm not attempting to learn
anything during that period.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.320
I just know that it's beneficial
to be listening to whatever

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:22.950
it is I'm listening to.

00:05:24.015 --> 00:05:28.905
But I try to have a certain period
of time and because I'm retired,

00:05:28.905 --> 00:05:29.885
it can be in the afternoon.

00:05:30.795 --> 00:05:35.205
Uh, I used to do it before going to
sleep, but that's a bit distracting

00:05:35.205 --> 00:05:38.925
and I, I don't think it's good for
the brain to be trying very hard to

00:05:38.925 --> 00:05:40.605
learn things just before going to bed.

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:44.535
And I think there is perhaps some truth
in the fact that, because I do a lot

00:05:44.535 --> 00:05:50.120
of my learning on the iPad, that having
that electronic, those signals coming at

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.950
you is probably not good for your sleep.

00:05:52.099 --> 00:05:56.930
So if I am going to study say Egyptian
Arabic or something, I will have, I have

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.510
a textbook, so or a book, a textbook.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.570
And so I will look at the book
before going to sleep, but I don't,

00:06:02.599 --> 00:06:05.450
I don't want to use my iPad before
going into that and going to sleep.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:09.320
But for some people that could be
a period of time where they are

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.810
able to focus and concentrate.

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:15.760
Uh, I have said that with my listening,
which I do first thing in the morning,

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.030
that's another part of my routine.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.800
I don't worry about my focus.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.730
Uh, whatever I'm able to pick
up from my listening is good.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.230
And I'll either be listening to my
mini story for the 35th time, or I'll

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:33.150
be listening to, uh, a news podcast
and, and getting something from that.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.070
I don't worry about my focus there.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.960
Um, this idea of, of distractions,
obviously, if we are still working

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.600
and we're interrupted and we're
checking our email or checking

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.385
our, uh, you know, messages,
obviously it's better to do that.

00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:50.414
You know, save it until the
end of the hour or save it.

00:06:50.565 --> 00:06:54.075
But these are things that so many
people have written about that you

00:06:54.075 --> 00:06:56.924
can't be constantly checking your
Twitter feed, constantly checking

00:06:56.924 --> 00:06:58.994
your emails or your messages.

00:06:59.145 --> 00:07:03.044
You're better off to save that for a
period of time, do it in a, you know, in

00:07:03.044 --> 00:07:08.294
a sort of a, a batch and then get back
so that your learning, your language

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:12.974
learning is uh, you know, a batch process
or spending time with your language,

00:07:13.215 --> 00:07:17.235
and then the other duties that you
have, then they occur at a certain time.

00:07:17.295 --> 00:07:21.344
Now I'm not saying anything there
that hundreds of other people haven't

00:07:21.344 --> 00:07:23.775
said, not everyone can do that.

00:07:23.805 --> 00:07:28.065
And obviously it's very tempting
to, uh, especially okay, the Olympic

00:07:28.065 --> 00:07:31.844
games, uh, you know, whatever
your country is, how did we do?

00:07:32.145 --> 00:07:36.075
In the middle of your studying, because
you, you can access and you go into.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.750
So, you know, we can say don't do that.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:40.860
And some people are going to do it.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:47.100
Nevertheless, part of it is how
genuinely motivating is whatever

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.050
you're reading or listening to.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.570
Uh, how committed are you to the
task at hand, if you're only casually

00:07:54.570 --> 00:08:00.495
committed to the task, you'll be more
easily um, you know, distracted, if,

00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:05.655
uh, if say you were, uh, you know, your
life depended upon learning something,

00:08:05.655 --> 00:08:09.015
you would be totally motivated to
learn that you would stay on the task.

00:08:09.525 --> 00:08:16.125
Uh, if, uh, you're very interested in
some aspect of Japanese, anime or whatever

00:08:16.125 --> 00:08:21.255
it might be or, or, uh, Persian history
then yeah I'm going to stay on task.

00:08:21.435 --> 00:08:23.715
If it's not interesting,
then I won't stay on task.

00:08:23.715 --> 00:08:28.260
So focus is also a function of
how relevant, how interesting, how

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:32.010
compelling, whatever content you have is.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.940
Now when you're speaking, of course,
I don't think focus is so much of an

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.870
issue, uh, because you're involved in a
conversation say with your tutor or your

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.250
tutor is going to ask you questions.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.690
You're going to have to
respond to the question.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.880
So focus there is not so much of an issue.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:49.980
Focus becomes a bigger issue when you're
involved in what could be considered

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.230
passive activities like listening
and reading and your mind wanders.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:59.400
And in today's day and age and the
internet, internet age, it's easy to be

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.890
distracted and allow your mind to wander.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:09.060
So, uh, you know, all I can say really
is in my own case, I am distracted.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.490
Uh, I think to some extent it's
a, it's a negative that goes with

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:18.000
all of the other advantages that
the internet age provides us.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.020
So I accept that.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:25.680
I think I beat off the, the impulse
to go and check a check up on my email

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.199
or my Twitter feed, uh, as best I can.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.300
And sometimes I succumb.

00:09:30.015 --> 00:09:34.185
But, you know, at the end of
the day, there's still a lot of

00:09:34.185 --> 00:09:36.135
focused learning that takes place.

00:09:36.315 --> 00:09:39.765
And I think we don't need to be
perfectionist when it comes to focus

00:09:40.155 --> 00:09:44.895
and just try our best to focus as
best we can using whatever methods

00:09:44.895 --> 00:09:47.115
work best with our lifestyle.

00:09:47.265 --> 00:09:49.785
So that's really all I have to
say on the subject of focus.

00:09:50.505 --> 00:09:51.625
So I hope that was useful.

00:09:52.105 --> 00:09:52.555
Bye for now.

